 },
 "title": "Mid-Depth Speech Representation Targets Maximize Invasive Brain-to-Speech Decoding",
 "abstract": "Many brain-to-speech (BTS) synthesis systems rely heavily on intermediate speech representations. However, no prior work has studied how to best leverage these features within a consistent and unified setting. In this paper, we address this gap by conducting a comprehensive comparison of acoustic and semantic representations for sEEG-to-speech decoding, focusing on self-supervised learning (SSL) layer hierarchies. We perform experiments holding frame rate, vocabulary size, quantization, and decoder architecture consistent across a dataset of Dutch speech, finding that semantically informed representations provide superior decoding performance. In particular, HuBERT units and a HuBERT-distilled codec consistently outperform reconstruction-based codecs, spectral representations, and source\u2013filter features. We also perform systematic layer sweeps across five different SSL backbones, discovering that optimal decoding performance always comes from using mid-depth layer representations rather than extremely deep or shallow layers. Furthermore, neither parameter scaling nor target-language pretraining yields significant performance gains, and pooling multiple adjacent layers does not reliably outperform a well-chosen single layer. Token confusions show that decoding accuracy varies across phonetic classes. Waveform decoding fails for most participants, but the few recognizable words come mainly from high-\u03ba participants, and decodability tracks the number of speech-responsive contacts. This points to electrode coverage of speech-production cortex as the main bottleneck."
}